The program itself is trivial — read three ints, run a staged validation, print a result code — so nearly all the translation effort went into the two places where C semantics are easy to get wrong:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m[38;5;10mscanf[0m partial-match behavior.[22m [38;5;10mscanf("%d %d %d", &x, &y, &z)[0m writes directly into the file-scope [38;5;10my[0m, which is initialized to [38;5;10m123[0m. If input supplies fewer than three integers, the unassigned variables keep their prior values, so [38;5;10m1[0m alone yields [38;5;10mx=1, y=123, z=0[0m and the "x == 1 but y != 2" branch. I reproduced this with [38;5;10mscan_int[0m calls that bail out in order, leaving later targets untouched, and modeled [38;5;10my[0m as a [38;5;10mstatic AtomicI32[0m initialized to 123 so the initializer is observable on the failure path. [38;5;10m%d[0m skips leading whitespace including newlines, accepts an optional sign, and pushes back the first non-digit byte — hence the one-byte-pushback [38;5;10mCStdin[0m reader rather than line-based input.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mInteger overflow.[22m glibc buffers the digits and calls [38;5;10mstrtol[0m, which saturates at [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m, then stores the result through an [38;5;10mint *[0m, truncating it. So [38;5;10m99999999999999999999[0m arrives as [38;5;10m-1[0m, not as a clamped [38;5;10mINT_MAX[0m. [38;5;10mscan_int[0m saturates into [38;5;10mi64[0m and then does [38;5;10mas i32[0m, matching that. This is UB in the C standard, so it is glibc-specific: on a libc that behaves differently, this line is where the two would diverge.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The staged [38;5;10mgoto fail[0m structure became an if/else chain that falls through to the shared "Operation failed" print, keeping error-check order and the 1/2/3 result codes intact. The success path returns early without that line, exactly as the C does. I preserved the C's behavior of ignoring the [38;5;10mscanf[0m return value entirely rather than treating short input as an error.[0m[0m
